ng elderhood while addressing health systems and the cultural perceptions of old age.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"06ee8d4c27ad5ae5d0431ab6187aa5997ca28915f3f3e33368dd21bbe73e79a7","summary":"Finalist for the Pulitzer Prize in General Nonfiction A New York Times Bestseller Longlisted for the Andrew Carnegie Medal for Excellence in Nonfiction Winner of the WSU AOS Bonner Book Award Winner of the 2022 At Home With Growing Older Impact Award As revelatory as Atul Gawande’s Being Mortal, physician and award-winning author Louise Aronson’s Elderhood is an essential, empathetic look at a vital but often disparaged stage of life. For more than 5,000 years, \"old\" has been defined as beginning between the ages of 60 and 70. That means most people alive today will spend more years in elderhood than in childhood, and many will be elders for 40 years or more. Yet at the very moment that humans are living longer than ever before, we’ve made old age into a disease, a condition to be dreaded, denigrated, neglected, and denied. Reminiscent of Oliver Sacks, noted Harvard-trained geriatrician Louise Aronson uses stories from her quarter century of caring for patients, and draws from history, science, literature, popular culture, and her own life to weave a vision of old age that’s neither nightmare nor utopian fantasy--a vision full of joy, wonder, frustration, outrage, and hope about aging, medicine, and humanity itself. Elderhood is for anyone who is, in the author's own words, \"an aging, i.e., still-breathing human being.\"","pageCount":467,"publicationYear":2019,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Social Science","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-elderhood-redefining-aging-transforming-medicine-reimagining-life-louise-aronson"}],"displaySummary":"At Home With Growing Older Impact Award As revelatory as Atul Gawande’s Being Mortal, physician and award-winning author Louise Aronson’s Elderhood is an essential, empathetic look at a vital but often disparaged stage of life. For more than 5,000 years, \"old\" has been defined as beginning between the ages of 60 and 70. That means most people alive today will spend more years in elderhood than in childhood, and many will be elders for 40 years or more. Yet at the very moment that humans are living longer than ever before, we’ve made old age into a disease, a condition to be dreaded, denigrated, neglected, and denied. Reminiscent of Oliver Sacks, noted Harvard-trained geriatrician Louise Aronson uses stories from her quarter century of caring for patients, and draws from history, science, literature, popular culture, and her own life to weave a vision of old age that’s neither nightmare nor utopian fantasy--a vision full of joy, wonder, frustration, outrage, and hope about aging, medicine, and humanity itself. Elderhood is for anyone who is, in the author's own words, \"an aging, i.e., still-breathing human being.\"","primaryTopic":"Medicine, Health & the Body","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Medicine & Public Health","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Medicine & Public Health","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Medicine & Public Health","mappedSubject":"Medicine & Public Health","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily discusses the medical and societal aspects of aging, redefining elderhood while addressing health systems and the cultural perceptions of old age.","id":"subject-evidence-book-elderhood-redefining-aging-transforming-medicine-reimagining-life-louise-aronson-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Social Science","mappedSubject":"Society & Culture","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-elderhood-redefining-aging-transforming-medicine-reimagining-life-louise-aronson","id":"subject-evidence-book-elderhood-red